  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "[#153] Goal and polarity now render as configurable glyphs on rich sinks (Markdown/ANSI/Unicode). A MetricChange<T> metric label carries a goal glyph (↑ higher-is-better / ↓ lower-is-better) and its Change cell a derived polarity glyph (✓/✗) instead of the (+)/(-) marker and good/bad word. A goal-annotated Change<V> (composite card row or element-table column) also trails a polarity glyph. A MultiSourceRow with a Goal adds a pairwise polarity glyph to each scalar column (compared to its previous populated column) — a per-period trend over an n-column series — plus the goal glyph on the label. Easy mode: override any symbol via MarkoutWriterOptions.Glyphs (defaults ↑/↓/✓/✗). Advanced mode: MarkoutWriterOptions.ComposeGlyph is a callback that composes each glyph onto its text — replace it with a word, integrate it into the cell, or condition on the GlyphSlot/GateStatus. A caller StatusLabel stays a word. [#154] A MultiSourceRow { Emphasis = MarkoutEmphasis.AtLeast(cut) | AtMost(cut) } bolds each scalar cell whose value clears the threshold in Markdown (**…**) — a declared \"which numbers matter\" rule that composes with the goal glyph/polarity; point the comparison at the bad side for an alarm. Plain text keeps the ASCII (-)/(+) marker and status word, and TSV/JSONL keep the direction/status slug words and stay unstyled, so machine-readable and non-Unicode output are unchanged.",
